    <!-- Educational Primer -->
    <string name="edu_title">Martian Science Primer</string>
    <string name="edu_q_elevation">What is Mars Elevation (Areoid)?</string>
    <string name="edu_a_elevation">Mars has no sea level. Planetary scientists define zero elevation as the MOLA Areoid: an equipotential gravitational surface with an average equatorial radius of 3,396.19 km, determined by Mars Global Surveyor.</string>
    <string name="edu_q_crater">How do Martian Craters form?</string>
    <string name="edu_a_crater">Impact craters formed by hypervelocity asteroid collisions over billions of years. Because Mars has a thin atmosphere and no plate tectonics, billions of years of impact history remain preserved.</string>
    <string name="edu_q_hirise">What is HiRISE?</string>
    <string name="edu_a_hirise">The High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) onboard the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter resolves surface features as small as 30 cm per pixel, enabling identification of rover tracks and sedimentary layering.</string>
    <string name="edu_q_dtm">What is a Digital Terrain Model (DTM)?</string>
    <string name="edu_a_dtm">A 3D raster representation of Martian surface topography, generated by stereo photogrammetry from dual orbital observations (such as HiRISE or HRSC stereo pairs).</string>

